\title{Ramsey Theory: Bounds and Constructions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-26}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a unified, fully rigorous development of finite two-colour Ramsey
theory built on a single primitive --- the \emph{arrow relation}
$n \rightarrow (s,t)$ --- and use it to establish both exact small Ramsey
numbers and general asymptotic bounds. We prove the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres
recursion and its binomial consequence $R(s+1,t+1) \le \binom{s+t}{s}$; the
three classical exact values $R(3,3) = 6$, $R(3,4) = 9$, and $R(4,4) = 18$;
the central binomial estimate $\binom{2k}{k} \le 4^k$ and the resulting
exponential diagonal bound $R(k+1,k+1) \le 4^k$; and the colour-swap symmetry
$n \rightarrow (s,t) \iff n \rightarrow (t,s)$. The exact values are pinned by
explicit extremal constructions --- the pentagon $C_5$ for $R(3,3)$, the
M\"obius ladder $C_8(1,4)$ for $R(3,4)$, and the Paley graph on $\mathbb{Z}/17$
for $R(4,4)$ --- each certified clique-free by exhaustive finite verification.
We also situate these results against the probabilistic lower bound, which shows
the diagonal Ramsey number is exponential in $k$ from both sides. Every theorem
stated here has been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Ramsey theory studies the principle that sufficiently large combinatorial
structures necessarily contain highly ordered substructures, regardless of how
their elements are partitioned. For graphs, the canonical statement concerns
two-colourings of the edges of a complete graph: given target clique sizes $s$
and $t$, every sufficiently large complete graph whose edges are coloured red
and blue must contain a red clique of size $s$ or a blue clique of size $t$. The
least size guaranteeing this is the \emph{Ramsey number} $R(s,t)$.

Two complementary problems define the field:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Upper bounds} (forcing results): proving that order is
\emph{unavoidable} at a given size. The principal tool is the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres
recursion (1935).
\item \textbf{Lower bounds} (constructions): exhibiting a colouring at the
largest possible size that \emph{avoids} the target cliques, thereby proving the
Ramsey number is strictly larger.
\end{enumerate}
An exact value $R(s,t) = n$ requires both: a forcing proof that
$n \rightarrow (s,t)$ and an extremal construction showing
$\neg\,(n-1) \rightarrow (s,t)$.

\section{Definitions and Framework}

\subsection{Colourings as graphs}

A two-colouring of the edges of a complete graph on vertex type $V$ is encoded
by a single simple graph $G$ on $V$: an edge of $G$ is \textbf{red}, and a
non-edge (an edge of the complement $G^{\mathsf c}$) is \textbf{blue}. A
\textbf{red clique} is a clique of $G$; a \textbf{blue clique} is a clique of
$G^{\mathsf c}$, equivalently an independent set of $G$. We write
$G.\IsNClique\ s\ S$ for the property that the finite set $S$ has cardinality
$s$ and induces a clique in $G$.

\subsection{The arrow relation}

\begin{definition}[Arrow relation]
For naturals $n, s, t$, the relation $\Arrows\ n\ s\ t$ (classically
$n \rightarrow (s,t)$) holds iff for every vertex type $V$, every simple graph
$G$ on $V$, and every finite vertex set $W \subseteq V$ with $|W| \ge n$, there
exists $S \subseteq W$ with $G.\IsNClique\ s\ S$ (a red $s$-clique) or
$S \subseteq W$ with $G^{\mathsf c}.\IsNClique\ t\ S$ (a blue $t$-clique).
\end{definition}

Quantifying over an arbitrary ambient vertex type together with a finite subset
$W$ bakes monotonicity directly into the definition and lets the two recursive
sub-calls of the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres step live on subsets of one common vertex
set. The Ramsey number is $R(s,t) = \min\{\, n : \Arrows\ n\ s\ t \,\}$.

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]
If $\Arrows\ n\ s\ t$ and $n \le n'$, then $\Arrows\ n'\ s\ t$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate: a vertex set of size $\ge n'$ has size $\ge n$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres Recursion and Binomial Bound}

\begin{theorem}[Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres step]
If $m \rightarrow (s, t+1)$ and $n \rightarrow (s+1, t)$, with $m, n \ge 1$,
then $(m+n) \rightarrow (s+1, t+1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $W$ be a vertex set with $|W| \ge m+n$ and $G$ a colouring. Pick $v \in W$.
Partition the other $|W| - 1 \ge m + n - 1$ vertices into red neighbours $R$ and
blue neighbours $B$ of $v$. Since $|R| + |B| \ge m + n - 1$, either $|R| \ge m$
or $|B| \ge n$. If $|R| \ge m$, apply $m \rightarrow (s, t+1)$ to $R$: a blue
$(t+1)$-clique finishes; else a red $s$-clique $S \subseteq R$ extends through
$v$ to a red $(s+1)$-clique. The case $|B| \ge n$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Trivial arrows]
For all $b$, $\Arrows\ 1\ 1\ b$; and for all $a$, $\Arrows\ 1\ a\ 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A non-empty vertex set contains a single vertex, simultaneously a red $1$-clique
and a blue $1$-clique.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres binomial bound]
For all naturals $s, t$,
\[
\Arrows\ \binom{s+t}{s}\ (s+1)\ (t+1), \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
R(s+1, t+1) \le \binom{s+t}{s}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Double induction on $s$ and $t$. The base cases use the trivial arrows and
$\binom{t}{0} = \binom{s}{s} = 1$. The inductive step uses Pascal's rule
$\binom{s+t}{s} = \binom{(s-1)+t}{s-1} + \binom{s+(t-1)}{s}$ to split the
threshold so that the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres step combines the two smaller
instances.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact Small Ramsey Numbers}

\subsection{$R(3,3) = 6$}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]
$\Arrows\ 6\ 3\ 3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The instance $s = t = 2$ of the binomial bound, since $\binom{4}{2} = 6$.
\end{proof}

The lower bound uses the \textbf{pentagon} $C_5$ on $\mathbb{Z}/5$, with
$a \sim b \iff a + 1 = b$ or $b + 1 = a$.

\begin{lemma}
$C_5$ has no red triangle, and $C_5^{\mathsf c}$ (also a $5$-cycle) has no blue
triangle.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Exhaustive finite check over all $\binom{5}{3} = 10$ triples.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound]
$\neg\,\Arrows\ 5\ 3\ 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$R(3,3) = 6$, i.e. $\Arrows\ 6\ 3\ 3 \wedge \neg\,\Arrows\ 5\ 3\ 3$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{$R(3,4) = 9$}

The binomial bound gives only $R(3,4) \le \binom{5}{2} = 10$. The sharp value
requires a parity refinement.

\begin{lemma}[Handshake]
For any colouring $G$ and finite set $W$,
\[
\sum_{v \in W} \bigl|\{\, w \in W \setminus \{v\} : G.\Adj\ v\ w \,\}\bigr|
\ \text{is even.}
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The sum counts ordered red pairs $(v,w)$ inside $W$; the fixed-point-free
involution $(v,w) \mapsto (w,v)$ shows it equals twice the number of red edges.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
If $v \in W$ has $\ge 4$ red neighbours in $W$, then $W$ contains a red triangle
or a blue $K_4$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Among $4$ red neighbours of $v$, either two are red-adjacent (a red triangle
with $v$), or all $\binom{4}{2}=6$ pairs are blue (a blue $K_4$).
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
If $v \in W$ has $\ge 6$ blue neighbours in $W$, then $W$ contains a red triangle
or a blue $K_4$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Apply $R(3,3) = 6$ to the $6$ blue neighbours: a red triangle finishes; a blue
triangle extends through $v$ to a blue $K_4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]
$\Arrows\ 9\ 3\ 4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In a counterexample on a $9$-set, the previous two lemmas force red-degree
exactly $3$ at every vertex, so the total red-degree is $9 \cdot 3 = 27$, odd,
contradicting the handshake lemma.
\end{proof}

The lower bound uses the \textbf{M\"obius ladder} $C_8(1,4)$ on $\mathbb{Z}/8$:
$a \sim b \iff a - b \in \{\pm 1, 4\}$.

\begin{lemma}
$C_8(1,4)$ has no red triangle, and its complement has no blue $K_4$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}
$R(3,4) = 9$, i.e. $\Arrows\ 9\ 3\ 4 \wedge \neg\,\Arrows\ 8\ 3\ 4$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{$R(4,4) = 18$}

\begin{theorem}[Colour-swap symmetry]
$\Arrows\ n\ s\ t \implies \Arrows\ n\ t\ s$; consequently
$\Arrows\ n\ s\ t \iff \Arrows\ n\ t\ s$ and $R(s,t) = R(t,s)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply $\Arrows\ n\ s\ t$ to the complement $G^{\mathsf c}$: a red $s$-clique of
$G^{\mathsf c}$ is a blue $s$-clique of $G$, and a blue $t$-clique of
$G^{\mathsf c}$ (a clique of $G^{\mathsf{cc}}=G$) is a red $t$-clique of $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]
$\Arrows\ 18\ 4\ 4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres step with $m = n = 9$, $s = t = 3$, combining
$9 \rightarrow (3,4)$ and (by symmetry) $9 \rightarrow (4,3)$ into
$18 \rightarrow (4,4)$.
\end{proof}

The lower bound uses the \textbf{Paley graph} on $\mathbb{Z}/17$. Since
$17 \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, the nonzero quadratic residues
$\QR_{17} = \{1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16\}$ form a negation-symmetric set, so
$a \sim b \iff a - b \in \QR_{17}$ defines a self-complementary graph.

\begin{lemma}
The Paley graph on $\mathbb{Z}/17$ has no red $K_4$, and its complement has no
blue $K_4$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound]
$\neg\,\Arrows\ 17\ 4\ 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$R(4,4) = 18$, i.e. $\Arrows\ 18\ 4\ 4 \wedge \neg\,\Arrows\ 17\ 4\ 4$.
\end{theorem}

\section{The Exponential Diagonal Bound}

\begin{theorem}[Central binomial estimate]
For all $k$, $\displaystyle \binom{2k}{k} \le 4^k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The central coefficient is one term of the full row sum:
\[
\binom{2k}{k} \le \sum_{i=0}^{2k} \binom{2k}{i} = 2^{2k} = 4^k. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential diagonal bound]
For all $k$, $\Arrows\ (4^k)\ (k+1)\ (k+1)$, i.e. $R(k+1, k+1) \le 4^k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The binomial bound with $s = t = k$ gives
$\Arrows\ \binom{2k}{k}\ (k+1)\ (k+1)$; the central binomial estimate plus
monotonicity raise the threshold to $4^k$.
\end{proof}

The bound is qualitatively correct (exponential) but loose for small $k$: it
yields $R(3,3) \le 16$ and $R(4,4) \le 64$, whereas the exact values are $6$ and
$18$.

\section{The Lower Bound Side: The Probabilistic Method}

\begin{theorem}[Counting lower bound]
If $2 \binom{n}{k} < 2^{\binom{k}{r}}$, then there exists an $r$-uniform
two-colouring of the complete $r$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices with no
monochromatic clique of size $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Colour each $r$-subset independently and uniformly. For a fixed $k$-set the
probability of being monochromatic is $2 \cdot 2^{-\binom{k}{r}}$; a union bound
over all $\binom{n}{k}$ such sets gives total probability
$\le 2\binom{n}{k}2^{-\binom{k}{r}}$. If this is $< 1$, an avoiding colouring
exists.
\end{proof}

For graphs ($r=2$) this gives $R(k,k) > 2^{k/2}$, which together with the
exponential upper bound brackets the diagonal Ramsey number between $2^{k/2}$
and $4^k$.

\section{Discussion and Future Directions}

The probabilistic method, self-complementary pseudo-random graphs such as the
Paley graph, parity/handshake obstructions, and the Hales--Jewett abstraction to
high-dimensional cubes all flow from the ideas above. Promising next steps
include pinning $R(3,5) = 14$ via a circulant on $\mathbb{Z}/13$, abstracting the
regularity--parity obstruction into a general sharpness criterion, formalizing
the probabilistic diagonal lower bound $R(k+1,k+1) > 2^{k/2}$ to bracket the
diagonal Ramsey number from both sides, and studying the necessity of
self-complementarity for extremal diagonal colourings.

\section{Conclusion}

From a single relational primitive we derived the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres recursion
and binomial bound, the exact values $R(3,3)=6$, $R(3,4)=9$, $R(4,4)=18$ via
explicit verified constructions, and the exponential diagonal bound
$R(k+1,k+1) \le 4^k$, unified by colour symmetry.

